Source: pamsignal
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Tuan Nguyen <anhtuank7c@hotmail.com>
# Use the legacy `debhelper (>= 12~)` + `debian/compat` (file containing "13")
# form rather than `debhelper-compat (= 13)`. The latter is a standalone
# virtual package only published on Ubuntu 22.04+ (debhelper 13.x);
# Ubuntu 20.04 ships debhelper 12.10 which supports compat 13 via the
# legacy file but not the virtual build-dep. The two forms are
# interchangeable on Jammy/Noble/Resolute, so this single declaration covers
# Focal + everything newer.
Build-Depends: debhelper (>= 12~),
               meson,
               ninja-build,
               pkg-config,
               libsystemd-dev,
               libcmocka-dev
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: pamsignal
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         curl,
         systemd
Pre-Depends: adduser
Description: Real-time PAM login monitor with multi-channel alerts
 PAMSignal follows the systemd journal for PAM authentication events from
 sshd, sudo, su and login. It reports successful and failed logins and
 session opens and closes, detects brute-force attempts (remote by source
 IP, local sudo/su by account) and a successful login that follows
 repeated failures, and sends alerts to Telegram, Slack, Microsoft Teams,
 WhatsApp, Discord, or a custom HTTPS webhook (ECS JSON, optional auth
 header and mTLS).
 .
 The daemon runs as an unprivileged system user with read access to the
 journal, ignores entries not written by the real sshd, sudo, su or login
 binaries, dispatches alerts through fork+exec of curl with credentials
 kept out of argv, and ships with systemd sandboxing (NoNewPrivileges,
 ProtectSystem=strict, SystemCallFilter, MemoryDenyWriteExecute, etc.).
